WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.860
Even as CBS’ "Mom" lost one of its biggest&nbsp;
stars, the show still pulled off a season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.684
finale viewers can be satisfied with. Here's how&nbsp;
the characters came through their darkest times.

00:00:09.684 --> 00:00:14.460
As a heart-warming sitcom that never gets&nbsp;
cheesy, "Mom" is a rare beast. This isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.020
an easy trick for a show that often features&nbsp;
multiple AA meetings per episode. A big part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.100
of what keeps the humor of "Mom" from being&nbsp;
hackneyed is the way it honors the recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.500
process. "Mom" understands the careful balance of&nbsp;
hope, grit, and focus necessary to staying sober.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.040
As it turns out, substance abuse is a&nbsp;
matter close to the heart of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.480
creative team. Cast member Kristen Johnston has&nbsp;
reflected on her own history with addiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.200
as per Today, and producer Chuck&nbsp;
Lorre has made it clear how important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.920
he believes the show's message is in&nbsp;
our modern world. Lorre told Variety.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.420
"Women helping women recover from the seemingly&nbsp;
hopeless disease of alcohol and drug addiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.380
provided this extraordinary ensemble of&nbsp;
great actresses […] In loving each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.880
and supporting each other, they survive not as&nbsp;
individuals—the me is overridden by the we."

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.400
That "we" encompasses the stacked ensemble cast of&nbsp;
"Mom," also includes Mimi Kennedy, Jaime Pressly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.560
Beth Hall, and William Fichtner. Every episode&nbsp;
uses hope and humor to navigate the lives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.120
women in recovery, and the final season is&nbsp;
no different. The show draws to a close by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.342
admitting the struggle is very real—but&nbsp;
so are the friends made along the way.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.460
While she isn't physically present in&nbsp;
Season 8 of "Mom," Christy still has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.180
relationship with Bonnie throughout it.&nbsp;
Bonnie even fondly jokes about Christy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.260
in the moving finale. Viewers get a sense&nbsp;
that Christy and Bonnie are on good terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.500
even though Christy is busy at&nbsp;
Georgetown Law. While this casual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.101
camaraderie might seem typical for a sitcom,&nbsp;
it's downright miraculous for the Plunketts.

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:39.221
"I don't like you, but we eat&nbsp;
eight meals a week together."

00:01:39.221 --> 00:01:43.260
Most of this show's humor comes from Christy&nbsp;
and Bonnie butting heads. The mother-daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.400
duo are an odd couple: Christy constantly&nbsp;
searches for approval and a steady path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.040
while Bonnie blazes a self-serving trail through&nbsp;
any and all situations. But Christy's meekness can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.420
easily shift to rage against Bonnie, just like&nbsp;
Bonnie's air of confidence can be rattled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.860
the slightest judgment from her daughter. These&nbsp;
leading ladies are a comedy dream team, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.720
as a vivid portrait of living and forgiving in&nbsp;
recovery. No matter how much progress either mom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.700
makes, they can always remind each other of the&nbsp;
consequences of their chaotic pasts. This keeps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.780
them on their toes and in check—when they aren't&nbsp;
screaming at each other or smuggling maple syrup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.440
across the Canadian border, anyway. But when&nbsp;
Christy takes the next step in her healing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.623
journey all the way to law school, she leaves&nbsp;
Bonnie to assume her own new role: den mother.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.180
Though "Mom" initially focuses on Christy, it ends&nbsp;
as an ensemble show led by Bonnie. While Bonnie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.020
loves the attention that comes with being the AA&nbsp;
gang's de facto leader, she doesn't always love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.820
the responsibility it requires. This is evidenced&nbsp;
when she lies her way into booking business for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.560
her and Tammy, bullies Marjorie into attending a&nbsp;
slumber party, and unintentionally makes everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.960
confront something traumatic from their past, and&nbsp;
mocks new sponsee Rod, played by Steve Valentine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.941
for not remembering a passionate weekend they&nbsp;
spent together during his rock star days.

00:02:52.941 --> 00:02:55.601
"You don't forget me. I forget you."

00:02:55.601 --> 00:03:00.060
But the ending of "Mom" shows Bonnie is the&nbsp;
glue holding her band of ladies together—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.560
a dauntless crusader. Season 8 sees her&nbsp;
get a sleazy strip club owner to take down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.420
a 20-year-old billboard of Christy, spur her&nbsp;
ADHD therapist into asking out an old crush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.700
and throw out her roast of Marjorie in favor&nbsp;
of a heartfelt speech. When Bonnie isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.462
terrifying Adam and the gals with her wrath,&nbsp;
she's helping them stay on track and grow.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.960
Allison Janney is one of the finest actors of our&nbsp;
time. Known for her work on "The West Wing," "I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.040
Tonya," and "10 Things I Hate About You,"&nbsp;
she won two Emmys for her performance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.940
"Mom." Janney can pull off heart-wrenching&nbsp;
moments of vulnerability and slapstick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.040
silliness—and on "Mom," both are often required&nbsp;
in lightning-quick scenes. It's only appropriate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.240
then, that her finale co-star is another&nbsp;
powerhouse performer: Melanie Lynskey.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.160
The "Mom" finale features the "Yellowjackets"&nbsp;
star as Shannon. Shannon is brand-new to recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.440
and mistrustful of Bonnie's shiny, happy AA&nbsp;
gang, who are in particularly smiley form at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.460
the start of the episode. When Bonnie clocks&nbsp;
that Shannon definitely isn't feeling it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.500
she chases after her through the rain, ruining&nbsp;
her new suede shoes in the process. This sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.760
isn't typical of the old Bonnie, but we get the&nbsp;
sense that it might be what the new-and-improved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.261
version is all about. Bonnie ultimately&nbsp;
convinces Shannon to get coffee with the gang.

00:04:10.381 --> 00:04:12.421
"What is it with you people and coffee?"

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:15.379
"It's the only drug we have left, so we abuse it."

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.680
These evenly matched actresses set a strong&nbsp;
and poetic tone for the end of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.762
and create a final showdown between&nbsp;
a stand-in mother and daughter.&nbsp;

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:28.142
"And life possibly changing in five, four, three."
"Don't do that voice."&nbsp;

00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:28.763
"Roger that."

00:04:29.205 --> 00:04:30.780
Bonnie sees a lot of herself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.040
Shannon—especially her bad attitude and sense&nbsp;
of being marked for failure. While we've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.600
Bonnie try to play the hero before, this time,&nbsp;
she manages to put her ego aside and calmly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.620
tell Shannon she can call any time she's&nbsp;
tempted to use. Just like the old Bonnie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.700
Shannon is convinced she doesn't need, or&nbsp;
deserve, real help, and brushes the offer off.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.440
But later, she comes begging for aid–just&nbsp;
as Bonnie, stressed by news of Adam's cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.640
diagnosis, struggles to give it. Bonnie is&nbsp;
rattled, and becomes even more so when Jill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.840
reveals she and Andy are getting married&nbsp;
right away and want Bonnie to attend. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.040
then Shannon's mom chases Shannon through a&nbsp;
window. When Bonnie can't get hold of Shannon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.620
a pit forms in her stomach. Has Shannon met&nbsp;
the same fate as Jodi? Chaos reigns—the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.240
kind that would have driven the old Bonnie to&nbsp;
drink. We've seen old Bonnie relapse before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.800
But this time, she doesn't. Instead, she&nbsp;
works hard to support Shannon and Adam.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.700
Bonnie ends up pulling some strings&nbsp;
to get the brawling Jolene and Shannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.240
contained at Jill and Andy's courthouse&nbsp;
wedding—and their first mother-daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.080
AA meeting. When Bonnie addresses&nbsp;
the room with her moving final share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.902
we see an echo of her and Christy in Shannon and&nbsp;
Jolene. This makes it clear how far she's come.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.560
While the AA meetings of "Mom" see&nbsp;
their fair share of guest stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.360
Marjorie is on the show from Episode 1. Mimi&nbsp;
Kennedy portrays the eccentric hippie with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.620
nuanced mix of tenderness and tenacity.&nbsp;
Marjorie is more than her love of cats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.160
and velour tracksuits—she's struggled&nbsp;
with addiction, feelings of failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.120
and motherhood. But with Bonnie and the&nbsp;
gang by her side, she gets a second chance.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.840
"Mom" celebrates Marjorie's long road to&nbsp;
reconnection with her son in the Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.060
8 episode "A Community Hero and a Wide Turn."&nbsp;
Marjorie is nominated for a leadership award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.680
and soon discovers her formerly-estranged&nbsp;
son nominated her. They share a touching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.380
full-circle moment, which is made&nbsp;
even more satisfying by the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.400
that Marjorie misses the awards ceremony&nbsp;
to provide support to a pregnant Jill.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.780
"Mom" also delivers another poignant moment&nbsp;
between Marjorie and sponsee Bonnie in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.400
series finale, "My Kinda People and the Big&nbsp;
To-Do." While Bonnie often makes cracks about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.540
Marjorie in the same breath as asking her for&nbsp;
help, in the finale, she's as straightforward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:35.160
as we've ever seen her. She reaches out in a time&nbsp;
of need, and Marjorie advises Bonnie that all she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.000
can do is "the next right action," leaving&nbsp;
the audience with a powerful final message.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:45.540
Comedy legend Kristen Johnston plays Tammy,&nbsp;
Bonnie's friend and business partner. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.860
first blush, you'd never guess Tammy held up a&nbsp;
restaurant on "Cops Eat Free" night, back when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.480
she was using. Tammy starts her journey on "Mom"&nbsp;
searching for a family that won't fracture. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.040
own father murdered her mother, leaving Tammy&nbsp;
to bounce around the foster care system. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.480
spent some formative years with Bonnie and a&nbsp;
few years in prison. But by Season 8 of "Mom,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.920
she's a far cry from the person she once was.&nbsp;
She has made amends with the steakhouse owner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.840
and even has a sweet and handsome limo driver&nbsp;
boyfriend. Tammy's story arc sees her learn how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.220
to find hope and joy without substances&nbsp;
and finally find a family worthy of her.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:22.080
Johnston is familiar with the recovery process.&nbsp;
She wrote a memoir about her experience, called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.420
"Guts: The Endless Follies and Tiny Triumphs of&nbsp;
a Giant Disaster." She told The New York Post,

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.140
"I love being sober and the recovery&nbsp;
community. It has given me such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:35.083
great gifts. And I count this job [on&nbsp;
'Mom'] as one of them, I really do."

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.840
No character provides as much extra comic&nbsp;
relief on "Mom" as the unspeakably rich Jill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.400
played by Jaime Pressly. Jill's vanity and&nbsp;
materialism are matched only by her desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.440
to prettify her own pain and her longing&nbsp;
to escape her haunted past. Jill blames her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.060
perfectionism for her bad luck in love, but&nbsp;
in the final season of "Mom," she gets more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:59.040
than she bargained for in that arena. Jill finally&nbsp;
allows herself genuine joy in the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.780
She's lived a lifetime masking her pain over her&nbsp;
mother's death, her trouble conceiving a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.100
her bittersweet fostering experience, and&nbsp;
her divorce. But while she often struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:12.180
with going with the flow of life, rather than&nbsp;
trying to control every inch of it, the finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:17.460
of "Mom" sees her accept herself and her life as&nbsp;
a joyful mess. She marries Andy in a courthouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:22.709
while visibly pregnant, as two grown women&nbsp;
brawl behind her—and she couldn't be happier.

00:08:22.709 --> 00:08:27.240
Wendy, played by Beth Hall, is perhaps the&nbsp;
greatest enigma on "Mom," and not all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.440
her mysteries are unraveled by the series&nbsp;
finale. Wendy's emotionality and randomness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.700
are stronger than any other character's on the&nbsp;
show, earning her many puzzled looks from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.380
gang across the AA meeting room. While it isn't&nbsp;
always apparent what's on Wendy's mind, her final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.221
season story arc sees her get out from under the&nbsp;
shadow of her friends and into her own light.

00:08:45.221 --> 00:08:46.541
"That's right, I'm funny."

00:08:46.541 --> 00:08:50.400
"Weeping Wendy" has learned to regulate&nbsp;
her emotions significantly by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.420
the series ends. Often the show's most&nbsp;
sidelined character, Wendy has learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.500
how to stand on her own two feet by the&nbsp;
finale, and help others learn to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.460
same footing. Instead of working herself to&nbsp;
death at the hospital and using substances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:06.480
to push through, Wendy closes the series&nbsp;
as a rock-steady leader of the program.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.080
While we're used to seeing Wendy in the&nbsp;
background of group shots, our final image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:15.822
of her is a woman who stands tall behind the AA&nbsp;
podium. She asks both the room and the audience,

00:09:15.822 --> 00:09:17.442
"Who else would like to share?"

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.440
Adam lived life on the wild side, until he lost&nbsp;
the use of his legs. As Bonnie's new husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.180
he lives on a different wild side. Adam is&nbsp;
the straight man to Bonnie's scheming clown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.740
and a charming, challenging, stabilizing&nbsp;
force. For all of Bonnie's lioness qualities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.560
her relationship with Adam&nbsp;
spotlights her tender underbelly.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.980
When Bonnie confronts her fear of&nbsp;
abandonment, deep-seated insecurities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.180
and potential for growth, it's often because&nbsp;
of Adam's encouragement—or because Bonnie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.400
in the midst of a panic spiral about his safety.&nbsp;
William Fichtner's pitch-perfect comic timing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.420
dry-as-a-bone delivery always sets Bonnie&nbsp;
to rights. But when Adam and Bonnie learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.100
of his cancer diagnosis in the finale, Adam is&nbsp;
suddenly the one in need of a stabilizing force.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.960
Bonnie, however shakily, steps up to support&nbsp;
Adam in this climactic moment. She's a rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.800
to her husband at the doctor's office—and&nbsp;
a blunt force object against his doctor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.340
poor fish tank cleaning standards. When she&nbsp;
insists they'll get through this together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.880
the otherwise numb Adam is truly&nbsp;
comforted. And she's going to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:16.003
sure the team takes whatever the next&nbsp;
right step is, over and over again.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.220
The ending of "Mom" mirrors its beginning,&nbsp;
and re-ups the show's thesis statement:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.840
Recovery is possible, even if relapse is&nbsp;
probable. "Mom" proposes that those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.740
desire redemption and rebirth through&nbsp;
recovery deserve it and can earn it if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.820
they work for it. They don't have to do it&nbsp;
alone, either. "Mom" finds recovery within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:36.840
supportive and loving communities. Bonnie&nbsp;
delivers a powerful share in the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.320
addressing her friends and the group's&nbsp;
dysfunctional new mother-daughter duo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.622
She admits that despite the reasons she could&nbsp;
have a drink, she doesn't. Why? She offers,

00:10:45.622 --> 00:10:50.741
"I kind of like me. I kind of love me."

00:10:50.741 --> 00:10:53.400
It's a moving distillation&nbsp;
of growth and acceptance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.600
and we see it across the show's beloved ensemble.&nbsp;
Though the finale leaves unanswered questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.642
we know Bonnie's going to be okay as long as she&nbsp;
keeps working for it—with help from her friends.

